{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And the man called the name, etc.</b> One must pay attention to why he did not call her a name at the first hour, before every deed that was done." "Know that this woman had already been elevated to praise, and HaShem called her a name together with her husband, as it says: ״וַיִּקְרָא אֶת שְׁמָם אָדָם״ (Genesis 5:2) — a name of praise and glory, for this name אָדָם is an exalted name, as the prophet said (Ezekiel 34:31): ״אָדָם אַתֶּם״, and they said, of blessed memory (Yevamot 61a): ״You are called אָדָם״ etc. <b>And now</b> there was no place for the man to call a name to Chavah, whereas after the deed — when she listened to the words of the inciter and acted willfully regarding the Tree of Knowledge — she descended from her level and lost the exalted name. And so this is the way in the world: whenever a person acts willfully his name is diminished, as it says (Proverbs 10:7): ״וְשֵׁם רְשָׁעִים יִרְקָב״ — may the Merciful One save us. Therefore the man came and called her a name based on her deed, that she was the mother of all life. And although the man too sinned, I have already written above that he was unwitting in the matter." "<b>Further,</b> he too experienced a descent for the time being, since Scripture calls him here ״הָאָדָם״ three or four times. And you should know that the level of ״הָאָדָם״ is lesser than the level of the name ״אָדָם״, as stated in tractate Avodah Zarah (Avodah Zarah 3a), and this is their language: Rabbi Yirmiyah said (the version in the Gemara is Rabbi Meir; however, the Tosafot in Sanhedrin 59a wrote ״and some explain that we read Rabbi Yirmiyah״ — a gloss) ״אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה אוֹתָם הָאָדָם״ etc. — priests and Levites are not stated, etc.; even a gentile who engages in Torah, etc. And Rashi and Tosafot raised a difficulty between this statement and the statement of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, who said: ״You are called אָדָם״ etc., and the Tosafot answered that there is a distinction and a superiority between ״אָדָם״ and ״הָאָדָם״, for ״אָדָם״ is the greater and the more praiseworthy, etc. And although Rashi, of blessed memory, wrote (Sanhedrin 59a) that the Tannaim disagree, with all this the words of the Tosafot are primary, for we find explicitly in the words of Rashbi that he distinguishes according to the words of the Tosafot. And the reason for the matter is that ״אָדָם״ is in its reckoning מ״ה (forty-five), and the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, is הֲוָיָ״ה with the filling of אַלְפִי״ן יַעֲלֶה כֵן, and with the addition of the letter הֵ״א the hinted intention is lost. And therefore He called him ״הָאָדָם״ after he sinned, but he did not remain in this descent, for we find that HaShem again called him ״אָדָם״, as it is written: ״וַיֵּדַע אָדָם עוֹד אֶת אִשְׁתּוֹ״ (Genesis 4:1), and he returned to his excellence, for his sin was unwitting." "<b>Further,</b> one may say a reason for the calling of a name after the entire deed. And first one must raise the question: why are living beings traced to her and not traced to the man, for he too is father of all life. One must further raise the question: according to the reason stated, she should have been called חַיָּה and not חַוָּה. However, the intention is that two things were renewed in her: first, that she listened to the words of the inciter, so he called her by his name — namely, his saying <b>חַוָּה</b>, from the word חִוְיָא, which is the serpent, and so too it is brought in the Zohar (Zohar Chadash, Genesis 19); and second, that it was decreed upon her to give birth in pain. Therefore he called her חַוָּה and did not call her חִוְיָא, to hint that she was the mother of all life, since she bears the suffering of pregnancy and of childbirth — it befitted her to be designated mother of all life, and not the man. And Scripture stated only a reason that is not tangible in the literal sense of the name, for the hint of חִוְיָא is recognizable in the name. Also, the juxtaposition of the matter to the deed of the serpent will indicate the thing."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {2 "The phrase הֲוָיָ״ה בְּמִלּוּי אַלְפִי״ן יַעֲלֶה כֵן refers to the divine name YHWH spelled out with the letter-filling aleph-lamed-pei, yod-aleph-lamed-heh, etc., yielding a gematria of 45 — matching אָדָם = 45. Translated hyper-literally as the text stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:15.750439700Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}